  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎻 Orchestra Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for orchestras, quartets and ensembles, plus titles for the concerts they play."
    },
    "intro": "Good orchestra names pair a town with a size of orchestra, from a <em>Sinfonia</em> to a full <em>Philharmonic</em>, while quartets and new-music groups pick a single striking word. This **orchestra name generator** invents believable places and pairs them with real orchestra types, and it also names ensembles, concert seasons and albums.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good orchestra names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Ashcombe Sinfonia</strong>: a town and a sinfonia, the usual name for a smaller orchestra.</li><li><strong>Oak Valley Philharmonic Orchestra</strong>: the full name for a full-size orchestra.</li><li><strong>Camerata Kingsmere</strong>: camerata, for a chamber group, put first in the Italian way.</li><li><strong>The Tarnbury Players</strong>: a modest, friendly name for an amateur orchestra.</li><li><strong>Corvus Quartet</strong>: <em>corvus</em> is Latin for crow: one striking word for a string quartet.</li><li><strong>Westmere Brass Quintet</strong>: a place and the exact line-up, so promoters know what they're booking.</li><li><strong>Kestrel Consort</strong>: consort is the old English word for a group of instruments.</li><li><strong>Pirates &amp; Adventurers: Music from the Movies</strong>: a family concert title that says what's on the programme.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Symphony, Philharmonic, Sinfonia: what the names mean",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Symphony</strong> and <strong>Philharmonic</strong> orchestras are the same size; <em>philharmonic</em> is Greek for \"loving harmony\". Cities with more than one orchestra use both, which is why London has the London Symphony Orchestra and the London Philharmonic.</li><li><strong>Chamber orchestra</strong> and <strong>sinfonia</strong> mean a smaller band, often of a few dozen players, as with the City of London Sinfonia.</li><li><strong>Camerata</strong> recalls the Florentine Camerata, a group of musicians and thinkers in late 16th-century Florence.</li><li><strong>Consort</strong> was the Elizabethan word for a group of instruments, and early-music ensembles still use it.</li><li><strong>Quartets</strong> often take one word, a person or a place: the Amadeus, Brodsky and Kronos Quartets.</li></ul><p>Choose <strong>Orchestra</strong> for a full or chamber orchestra, <strong>Ensemble or quartet</strong> for smaller groups like <em>Red Cedar Piano Trio</em>, and <strong>Concert or album</strong> for season and programme titles like <em>Midsummer Dances</em> or <em>Halloween Spooktacular</em>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming your orchestra",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Lead with the place</strong> if you're a community or youth orchestra. It's how audiences and funders will look for you.</li><li><strong>Use the right size word.</strong> A twenty-player group calling itself a Philharmonic sets expectations it can't meet.</li><li><strong>For concerts,</strong> name the programme or the occasion: a composer and a season, a theme, or the films you're playing.</li></ul><p>No orchestra yet? Plenty of composers start with samples: Composer HQ compares <a href=\"https://composerhq.app/sample-libraries\">orchestral sample libraries</a> and lists <a href=\"https://composerhq.app/courses/orchestration\">orchestration courses</a> for learning to write for the real thing.</p><p>Writing for your orchestra? The <a href=\"composer-name-generator.html\">Composer Name Generator</a> names composers and studios, and the <a href=\"score-title-generator.html\">Score Title Generator</a> names film and game cues. For the singers, try the <a href=\"choir-name-generator.html\">Choir Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more genres, see all the <a href=\"../categories/music_genres-names.html\">music genre name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between a symphony and a philharmonic orchestra?",
        "answer": "Nothing in size or instruments. Both are full orchestras; the different names usually just tell apart two orchestras in the same city, like the London Symphony and London Philharmonic."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you name a community orchestra?",
        "answer": "Put the town first and the kind of orchestra second, like Ashcombe Sinfonia or Oak Valley Philharmonic Orchestra. Choose a size word that matches how many players you have."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a sinfonia?",
        "answer": "A smaller orchestra, usually around chamber-orchestra size. The word is Italian for symphony, and orchestras like the City of London Sinfonia use it in their names."
      }
    ]
  }
